%[text] %[text:anchor:T_BB73E10C] # Checking Your Work 
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
%[text] As you learned in your calculus courses, the set of rules for computing derivatives of standard functions and their combinations is comprehensive. At the same time, that is definitely not the case for computing the *integrals* of standard functions and their combinations. Consider the difference between trying to express $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{-x^2}\\right)${"altText":"\frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{-x^2}\right)"} and $\\int e^{-x^2}\\; dx${"altText":"\int e^{-x^2}\; dx"}. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:6f63)
%[text] The Symbolic Math Toolbox offers a straightforward way to compute "back-of-the-book" answers so you can check yourself on involved computations while learning.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"} %[text:anchor:M_597CA885]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Before you get started:**
%[text] This live script is intended to be used with the code visible and output inline. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB Toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Output Inline**. Alternatively, select **Output Inline** using the icon ![live script output inline icon](text:image:55ed) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:95e5) Interacting with this live script will build some familiarity with MATLAB as concepts and commands are introduced together. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB, and [Symbolic Math Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/introduction-to-symbolic-math-with-matlab/symbolic), which teaches the basics of the Symbolic Math Toolbox.
%[text] To run the code, remember that you can use ![Run Section icon](text:image:1580) **Run Section** or ![Run and Advance icon](text:image:3a87) **Run and Advance** in the Live Editor tab of the toolstrip, or the equivalent keyboard commands of Ctrl-Enter to run a section or Ctrl-Shift-Enter to run and advance.
%[text] ## Defining a differential equation in MATLAB
%[text] Say you want to solve the differential equation 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $x''-5x'+6x = 7t^3-2${"altText":"x''-5x'+6x = 7t^3-2"}.
%[text] **Step 1:** Define the independent and dependent variables in your problem as symbolic variables using the command [`syms`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/syms.html)
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] syms t x(t)     % declare the independent variable t and the dependent variable x
%[text] ```

%[text] **Step 2:** Then use the command [`diff`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.diff.html) to define the differential equation using your symbolic variables.
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % Pick a name for your differential equation such as MyEquation
%[text] % and remember that symbolic equality is denoted by ==
%[text] MyEquation = diff(x,t,2) - 5*diff(x,t) + 6*x == 7*t^2 - 2;
%[text] ```

%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:962a) **Pro-tip**. This implementation of symbolic differentiation is the same for single-variable functions or multivariable functions, so the printed results will appear with partial derivative notation. There is another [`diff`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.diff.html) command in MATLAB that computes the numerical derivative of elements in an array. Which function is called depends on what values you pass into the command.
%[text] When you are working with symbolic variables, you may encounter this pop-up:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Suggested Next Steps Available\nView symbolic suggestions for next steps by clicking this icon or right-clicking the symbolic output. Checkbox: Don't show again. OK](text:image:9aa2)
%[text] If it bothers you, you can select "Don't show again", but it will offer a set of symbolic tools you may want to revisit later.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Solving a differential equation in MATLAB
%[text] Use the command [`dsolve`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/dsolve.html) to compute the symbolic solution.
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % To use solve a differential equation called MyEquation and assign the solution to the variable MySolution
%[text] MySolution = dsolve(MyEquation)
%[text] 
%[text] % Alternately, you can combine the steps and define-and-solve the differential equation in one step
%[text] MySolution = dsolve(diff(x,t,2)-5*diff(x,t)+6*x == 7*t^3-2)
%[text] ```

%%
%[text] ### With initial conditions
%[text] To define initial conditions, you must start by giving a name to any required functions. Let's say that $x(0) = 3${"altText":"x(0) = 3"} and $x'(0) = -1${"altText":"x'(0) = -1"}. We have already defined `x(t)`, but for a second-order problem we will also need to define $x'(t)${"altText":"x'(t)"}.
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] xprime = diff(x,t)                   % Define the required derivative as a symbolic function
%[text] cond = [x(0) == 3, xprime(0) == -1]  % Define the initial conditions 
%[text] MySolution = dsolve(MyEquation,cond) % Pass both the differential equation and the initial conditions to dsolve
%[text] ```



%%
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:0cb3) **Cautionary Note:** Remember that you may see [functions you don't recognize](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/airy.html), or solutions in terms of integrals:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % This result involves Airy functions and Gamma functions
%[text] cond = [x(0)==1; x(1) == 0];
%[text] MyUnexpectedSolution = dsolve(diff(x,t,2)-t*x==0,cond)
%[text] 
%[text] % The result cannot be simplified
%[text] MyNonSolution = dsolve(diff(x,t) == exp(-t^3))
%[text] ```

%[text] because it is easy to create simple-to-state differential equations that do not have analytical solutions. In this case, you may want to do further research or consider numerical methods of solution.
%%
%[text] ## Solving an algebraic equation in MATLAB
%[text] In some cases, you may want to solve an algebraic equation. Consider the classic quadratic equation
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ax^2+bx+c=0${"altText":"ax^2+bx+c=0"}.
%[text] The solutions are defined by the quadratic formula.
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % Define the symbolic constants a, b, c and variable x
%[text] syms a b c x
%[text] % Use the function solve to find the roots of the quadratic equation in terms of its coefficients 
%[text] QuadraticEquation = a*x^2+b*x+c==0
%[text] Roots = solve(QuadraticEquation)
%[text] ```





%%
%[text] ## Displaying results in MATLAB
%[text] The command [`displayFormula`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/displayformula.html) can be helpful for printing the results of your computation:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] displayFormula(['"My differential equation is: "' "MyEquation"; ...
%[text]     '"and the general solution is: "' "MySolution"])
%[text] 
%[text] % Visualize the result over the range t=0 to t=1.2
%[text] fplot(t,MySolution,[0 1.2])
%[text] ```



